function:
The opening prepares the intended audience for the integrated message expressed in the thesis.

Its functions may include:

• activating relevant prior understanding;

• establishing the context needed to understand the thesis;

• locating the paragraph within a larger question, problem, situation, or conversation;

• helping the audience recognize what issue the paragraph will address;

• creating continuity between what the audience presently understands and what the writer intends to communicate;

• preparing the audience to understand why the thesis is relevant or worthy of consideration when that preparation is needed.

The opening prepares the reader for the communicative act.

It does not replace the thesis or perform the elaborative work that follows.

discovery_instruction:
Compass teaches:

• what an opening is;

• why openings prepare a particular audience rather than merely begin a paragraph;

• how openings depend upon audience knowledge, communicative purpose, the larger conversation, and the thesis;

• how an opening differs from a thesis and from elaboration;

• why openings are optional rather than universally required;

• why hooks and attention-getting devices are possible strategies rather than canonical requirements;

• how to determine what orientation the intended audience genuinely needs.

The central discovery question is:

“What does this audience need before the thesis can accomplish its communicative purpose?”

Compass compares the learner’s opening with the structural requirements and invites the learner to determine what preparation is necessary.

Compass teaches the function and constraints of openings rather than prescribing a preferred opening technique.

Compass never writes the learner’s opening.
